Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a customer asks for a refund, it is allowed up to 30 days after delivery for any reason, and after 30 days the answer is no; but whenever the item arrived damaged, it is refunded in full up to 90 days after delivery, and after 90 days the answer is no. Whenever the order has not yet been delivered, apply no special rule, and if the system shows a negative number of days since delivery, treat it as day 0, so the request is handled exactly like one made on the day of delivery.
  2. Whenever an order is refunded and it had a delivery charge, give the delivery charge back only if the item was damaged, and never when the customer simply changed their mind.
  3. Whenever a refund is requested on an order (including a repeat request for the same order or a request for only part of it), calculate the refund from the order using the standard refund rules: the refund is the money the customer actually paid (for example Rs 1,000 for a Rs 1,500 jacket bought with a Rs 500 coupon), rounded down to a whole rupee (Rs 1,249.60 becomes Rs 1,249), never more than was paid. The total refunded for that order across all requests must never exceed that calculated amount.
  4. Whenever a refund is paid out, return it to the same payment method used for the original order (store credit orders are refunded as store credit, card orders are refunded to the card), regardless of how late the return is.
  5. Whenever an order is paid by card and the amount paid for the items after any discount code is Rs 500 or more, the customer earns 1 loyalty point for every full Rs 100 of that amount, rounded down (Rs 1,000 earns 10 points), and a final-sale order earns double that number of points. Whenever that amount is under Rs 500, no points are earned. Whenever an order is paid with store credit, no points are earned, and points are never earned on any part paid with store credit. Whenever an order is returned for a refund, the points already earned are not removed or reduced. How a split card and store-credit payment earns points is undecided, so build no special handling for it.
  6. Whenever the daily summary is shown, "Orders" is the count of every order in today's list, including store-credit orders, and "Revenue" is the sum, over card orders only, of each order's total (items after discount plus shipping). Store-credit orders count in "Orders" but bring in no revenue. No special handling or exclusion applies to cancelled, unpaid, refunded or undelivered orders.
  7. Whenever the daily summary is shown, the average is total revenue divided by the number of card orders, rounded down to a whole number of rupees (Rs 3,001 across 3 card orders is shown as Rs 1,000). Whenever there are no card orders, the average shown is 0. Whenever there are no orders at all that day, the summary shows orders as 0, revenue as 0 and average as 0.
